--- Finding: a list literal holding an int past 64 bits that the program
--- computes (`[2 ^ 65, 3]`, or `[a, 3]` with `a = 2 ^ 65`) stops where it
--- is made, in both modes, though a list holds any int as it is.
---
--- Interpreted and compiled (both):
---     runtime error: line 22: a whole number past 64 bits where an int is held in 64 bits: 36893488147419103232
--- Expected (spec, Numbers That Keep Every Digit: "What shows or holds any
--- value takes one as it is — str, log, text, type, a list's items …";
--- "A literal list of whole numbers that holds one growing past 64 bits is
--- a list of ints"):
---     [36893488147419103232, 3]
---     [3, 18446744073709551616, 36893488147419103232]
---
--- `crow build --ir`: `let xs: Tensor(Int, [2]) = [small(a), 3]` — the
--- literal is typed a tensor of ints and each item is narrowed to 64 bits,
--- although the width pass knows `a` is a growing int (`let a: Big`). The
--- same digits written as numerals, `[36893488147419103232, 3]`, make a
--- list of growing ints and run; so does a collecting loop
--- (`for k in [65, 1, 64]` / `2 ^ k`). `sort[[2 ^ 65, 3, 2 ^ 64]]` and
--- `concat[xs, [2 ^ 66]]` stop the same way, at the literal.
a = 2 ^ 65
xs = [a, 3]
log[xs]
log[sort[[2 ^ 65, 3, 2 ^ 64]]]
